  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThrough Tuesday: Heat is becoming the main forecast focus as \nmid/upper ridging begins to build across the Southern Plains and \nLower Mississippi Valley. High temperatures over the next few days\nwill begin to reach the upper 90s across the area to near 100 \nacross the ArkLaMiss Delta. Heat indices will reach the 105-110F \nrange especially over the western half of the area, thus a heat \nadvisory is in effect today for these areas. Additional \nadvisories may be needed beyond today. \n\nWhile the main upper trough axis that helped organize convection \nacross northern portions of the area on Saturday has begun to \nshift east, remnants of this feature remain across central/ \nsouthern sections of the forecast area and this is the most \nlikely focus for diurnal development later today. A few of these \nstorms could again produce strong wind gusts. By Monday and \nTuesday, convective coverage is expected to be more isolated as \nthe ridge strengthens. \n\nWednesday through next Sunday: Low level flow will become more \neasterly over the latter half of the week, ushering in a \nrelatively drier airmass, especially by Thursday. This airmass \nwill still support hot days, but with dewpoints dropping into 60s \nand perhaps the 50s, this will notably reduce heat stress danger \nthrough the end of the week. While days will still be relatively \nwarm, mornings will see temps dipping into the 60s more often \nacross much of the area. Deep layer moisture will begin to \nincrease again around Friday into Saturday, with returning \npotential for diurnal convection each day. \n\nLong range guidance has been hinting at a potential shift the\nfollowing week toward temps closer to daily normals, with perhaps\nhighs only in the 80s and lows in the 60s in some areas...not a \nreal taste of fall, but perhaps finally an end to the frequent \nspells of excessive heat? We're keeping our fingers crossed, stay\ntuned. /DL/",
